WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Clinical research is governed by two
complementary pillars, reflective ethics

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.180
and mandatory law. Ethics is reflective.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.940
It guides researchers based on cultural
values, DEI principles,

4
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.900
and the internal assessment of a study's
legitimacy.

5
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.600
This reflective process is how scientists
determine if their research is

6
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.260
fundamentally valid and reliable before it
reaches the public.

7
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.780
Conversely, the law is mandatory,
establishing strict rules, prohibitions and

8
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.000
procedures enforced by the state.

9
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.660
While ethics appeals to professional
conscience, the law protects human life

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.480
and fundamental rights through the force
of the state.

11
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.640
These two forces meet in the Research
Ethics Committee, or REC.

12
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.320
This multidisciplinary body is responsible
for evaluating the ethical and

13
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.740
scientific merit of every project.

14
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.320
International standards dictate that
research on humans may only proceed with

15
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.520
the explicit approval of a competent REC.

16
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.340
The REC translates reflective ethical
principles into a mandatory legal

17
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.380
authorization.

18
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:11.120
Because the field of AI evolves so rapidly,
our ethical norms must be dynamic.

19
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.380
They require constant reassessment to
remain effective.

20
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.360
Any future research utilizing AI must
demonstrate that it is valid,

21
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.760
reliable, legitimate, and representative
of those it intends to serve.

22
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.160
Maintaining legitimacy requires three
pillars of action.

23
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.720
First, we must monitor AI data life cycles
to ensure integrity from collection

24
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.640
to disposal. Second, we must enforce
fourth-generation identity rights,

25
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.860
making digital protection a standard
operating procedure.

26
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.020
Third, every study must ensure strict REC
authorization at every stage of the

27
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.620
process. Neglecting these foundational
pillars ultimately results in research

28
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.092
that is legally and ethically invalid,
regardless of its scientific results.
